  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000124366,
      "SummaryText": "Accidental Release Prevention and Emergency Response Policies:\nThe Broadway Self Storage LPG Bulk Plant (AKA South Portland Propane Terminal) facility is committed to ensuring the safety of our employees, the surrounding community, and the environment. We adhere to strict safety protocols and industry best practices to prevent accidental releases of propane. Our facility complies with all applicable federal, state, and local regulations, including the Risk Management Program (RMP) requirements under 40 CFR Part 68.\n\nWe have implemented a comprehensive emergency response program that includes coordination with local emergency responders, regular training for employees, and the use of advanced safety equipment. Our goal is to minimize the risk of accidental releases and to respond effectively in the unlikely event of an incident.\n\nFacility Description and Regulated Substances Handled:\nOur facility is a propane storage and distribution center that receives propane via rail cars and ships propane by truck. The facility includes:\n- Railcar unloading area: Equipped with safety systems to prevent overfilling and detect leaks.\n- Storage tanks: Pressurized tanks designed to safely store propane.\n- Truck loading area: Designed for safe and efficient transfer of propane to trucks.\nThe regulated substance handled at this facility is propane, which is classified as a flammable gas under 40 CFR Part 68. Propane is stored in quantities exceeding the threshold quantity of 10,000 pounds, making the facility subject to RMP requirements.\n\nGeneral Accidental Release Prevention Program:\nOur facility complies with the Program 3 prevention program requirements under 40 CFR Part 68, as well as other OSHA, NFPA, and state requirements. Key elements include:\n- Process safety information: Comprehensive documentation of propane properties, equipment specifications, and safety systems.\n- Process hazard analysis (PHA): Regularly conducted to identify and mitigate potential hazards.\n- Operating p"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000124366,
      "SummaryText": "rocedures: Detailed procedures for safe operation and maintenance of equipment.\n- Training: Regular training for employees on safe handling of propane and emergency response.\n- Mechanical integrity: Routine inspection and maintenance of equipment to ensure reliability.\n\nFive-Year Accident History:\nOver the past five years, our facility has had no accidental releases of propane that resulted in offsite impacts such as injuries, fatalities, or property damage.\n\nEmergency Response Program:\nOur emergency response program is designed to protect employees, the community, and the environment in the event of an accidental release. Key components include:\n- Coordination with local responders: Regular communication and joint training exercises with the local fire department and emergency management agencies.\n- Community Emergency response plan and Facility Emergency Action Plan: Include procedures for evacuation, shelter-in-place, and communication with the public.\n- Training and drills: Employees participate in regular emergency response drills to ensure readiness.\n\nPlanned Changes to Improve Safety:\nWe are committed to continuous improvement in safety. Planned changes include:\n- Evaluating leak detection systems to enhance early detection of propane releases.\n- Enhancing response planning with local agencies as RMP rules become finalized.\n- Conducting additional training sessions for employees when new safety technologies become available."
    }
  ]
